  "answer_core": "I would not grant Marcus 15% equity, a CTO title, and a board seat on Friday. Those are irreversible governance and ownership commitments, and you do not yet have evidence that the partnership reduces concentration risk rather than increasing it.\n\nSeveral dependencies currently run through him: the platform prototype, technical delivery, Jake and Lina, client confidence, and part of the exit story. If you grant the full package now, you price contribution and concentration risk before knowing whether he strengthens the company beyond himself.\n\nFriday should be a direct, structured conversation about a staged path, not a vague delay or flat rejection. A vague 'later' can read as avoidance and trigger exactly what you are trying to prevent: Marcus disengaging, pulling Jake and Lina with him, slowing the platform, or destabilizing client continuity. Go in ready to say you want to explore a serious path, but you cannot make informal promises that function as public commitments before there is written evidence and board process to support them.\n\nDefine a short, defined sprint—think weeks, not quarters—that tests whether he can deliver the key platform work, whether that work matters for revenue, whether team continuity improves rather than worsens, whether knowledge transfer happens, and whether his governance behavior fits what the company needs. Spell out what success in that window would unlock, and keep intermediate structures alive. Do not let the conversation turn into a catalog of instruments.\n\nUse exit math only as false-precision caution. Until buyer assumptions, platform value, and departure scenarios are tested, the numbers should not carry the decision. The goal is to avoid both a premature commitment and a vague delay that pushes him away. Give him a real path forward, but one tied to demonstrated impact on the company.",
